+# Install device plugins and validate hardware access
+
+1. NVIDIA GPUs
+ - Install NVIDIA GPU Operator or the nvidia-device-plugin daemonset (ops/hw/device_plugins/nvidia-device-plugin.yaml).
+ - Ensure nodes have NVIDIA drivers installed (use GPU node images or driver DaemonSet).
+ - Validate: kubectl get nodes -o json | jq '.items[].status.allocatable["nvidia.com/gpu"]'
+
+2. AWS Inferentia / Trainium
+ - Install AWS Neuron device plugin (ops/hw/device_plugins/aws-neuron-daemonset.yaml) or use vendor image.
+ - Ensure node AMIs include neuron-runtime and neuron drivers.
+ - Validate: /opt/neuron presence and device plugin exposes neuron resources.
+
+3. Cloud TPUs (GCP)
+ - TPUs are typically provided as TPU VMs; follow GCP TPU VM setup and network access guidance.
+ - For integrations with Kubernetes, consider TPU operator or expose TPU service endpoints to cluster.
+
+4. Graphcore IPU
+ - Install Graphcore device plugin (vendor-provided) and ensure Poplar SDK & drivers installed on nodes.
+
+5. Habana Gaudi
+ - Install Habana HCX and device plugin; ensure the kernel modules and runtime are present on nodes.
+
+6. Edge NPUs
+ - Install vendor plugin (EdgeTPU, etc.) and ensure driver runtime is present.
+
+Validation
+ - Run the async batched inference server image for the given hardware and execute perf tests (ops/ci/load_test_async.py or kubernetes job templates).
+ - Use the detection heuristics in ops/hw/backend_adapters.detect_backend() to check runtime availability.
+
